# Runs page JS — fetch and render both tables

## Context

Task 3 added the analytics-runs markup and the analytics details modal to the
Runs page; Task 2 made `GET /dtwin/metrics/history` return every version's
runs. This change writes the script side: `domain-runs.js` now populates both
stacked tables and drives the new modal, which makes the feature functional
end to end. The repository has no JavaScript test runner (no jest, no vitest,
no `.test.js` anywhere), so the tests read the script as text and assert on
markers in its source — the established fallback in this repo, precedent
`tests/units/settings/test_analytics_job_toggle.py`. The runtime-correctness
checks that no test can perform were done by reading (see below).

## Changes

1. `src/front/static/domain/js/domain-runs.js` — Split loading into two
   isolated loaders, `_loadBuildRuns()` and `_loadAnalyticsRuns()`, each
   owning its own loading / empty / error elements and its own `try` block.
   `loadDomainRuns()` stays the single global entry point (the Refresh
   button's `onclick` and both bottom-of-file event listeners call it) and
   now awaits the two loaders sequentially. Sequential awaits rather than a
   combinator that settles on first rejection: if one endpoint fails the
   other table must still render.
2. `src/front/static/domain/js/domain-runs.js` — Added the analytics
   renderers: `_analyticsRunRow()` emits exactly 11 `<td>` cells matching the
   markup's 11 headers (Date & Time, Scope, Version, Status, Nodes, Edges,
   Components, Avg Degree, Density, Duration, Details), and
   `_renderAnalyticsRunsTable()` fills `#analyticsRunsTableBody` from
   `_analyticsRunsCache`. Failed runs dash out their metric cells: the store
   records zeros for a run that died, and printing them would read as a graph
   with no nodes rather than a run that produced no numbers.
3. `src/front/static/domain/js/domain-runs.js` — Added
   `_analyticsStatusBadge()` alongside the existing `_statusBadge()`.
   Analytics runs report `completed`/`failed` where builds report
   `success`/`error`/`cancelled`, so overloading one helper would render every
   analytics row as an unknown-status grey badge.
4. `src/front/static/domain/js/domain-runs.js` — Added `_runLocalName()`,
   `_analyticsScope()` and `_fmtMillis()`. The local-name helper carries a
   `_run` prefix rather than being called `_localName`: this file declares its
   helpers at global scope and `query-chat.js`, loaded on the same page, has a
   `_localName` of its own. That one currently sits inside an IIFE so there is
   no clash today, but the prefix guarantees there never can be.
5. `src/front/static/domain/js/domain-runs.js` — Added
   `showAnalyticsRunDetails(idx)` (exported on `window`) rendering the
   analytics modal into `#analyticsRunDetailsBody`: status/version/when/
   duration summary, the error alert when present, the full scope URI list,
   and the graph metrics with the same failed-run dashing as the table.
6. `src/front/static/domain/js/domain-runs.js` — Deleted
   `_populateRunsVersions()` and the `_runsVersionSel` module state, and
   dropped the `data.versions` / `data.current_version` handling from the
   build loader. The `#runsVersionFilter` dropdown was removed from the
   template in Task 3, so this wiring was reading a non-existent element and
   filtering against a value that could never change. `_renderRunsTable()`
   now reads `_runsCache` directly and its `forEach` index is the cache index
   (previously it recovered the index via `indexOf`, which was only needed
   because the filtered row list diverged from the cache).
7. `tests/units/front/test_runs_page.py` — Added `TestRunsScript` with five
   source-marker tests: both endpoints are fetched; the two loads are
   independent (no rejection-combinator, exactly one of each loader); the
   analytics status badge is its own helper; the version-dropdown wiring is
   gone; and the failed-row renderer exists.

## Runtime checks performed by reading (no test covers these)

- **Element ids.** Every `document.getElementById(...)` in the file was
  matched against the committed markup: the six build ids and six analytics
  ids all exist in `partials/domain/_domain_runs.html`; `runDetailsModal` /
  `runDetailsBody` and `analyticsRunDetailsModal` / `analyticsRunDetailsBody`
  all exist at page level in `dtwin.html`; `runs-section` exists in both
  `dtwin.html` and `domain.html`.
- **Helper scope.** Every helper called is a top-level function declaration in
  the same file, so all are hoisted and in scope regardless of call order.
  `_analyticsRunsCache` is a module-level `let` read only from inside
  functions, so no temporal-dead-zone hazard.
- **Global name collisions.** `rg` across `src/` confirms none of the new
  global names (`_analyticsStatusBadge`, `_runLocalName`, `_analyticsScope`,
  `_fmtMillis`, `_analyticsRunRow`, `_renderAnalyticsRunsTable`,
  `_loadBuildRuns`, `_loadAnalyticsRuns`, `showAnalyticsRunDetails`,
  `_analyticsRunsCache`) is defined anywhere else. The other `_localName`
  definitions on the same page (`query-chat.js`, the inline script in
  `partials/dtwin/_query_analytics.html`, lines 379-1552 of which are one
  IIFE) are all scoped, and the new helper is prefixed anyway.
- **Dead-reference sweep.** `rg` confirms `_populateRunsVersions` and
  `_runsVersionSel` had no callers outside this file. `showRunDetailsObj`
  keeps its name, signature and `window` export, so `domain-audit.js:211`
  still works.
- **Cell count.** `_analyticsRunRow` was counted by hand against the markup:
  11 `<td>` for 11 `<th>`.

## Deviation from the brief

The brief's comment above `loadDomainRuns()` contained the literal string
`Promise.all`, which its own `test_the_two_fetches_are_independent` asserts is
absent from the file — copying it verbatim made that test fail. The comment
was reworded to say the same thing ("a combinator that settles on the first
rejection") without the forbidden literal.

