## Unconsumed-fields proof (Part C)

Searched for any reader of `versions` / `current_version` scoped to the
`/domain/build-runs` response specifically (both are common field names
reused by other endpoints, so a bare-word search would false-positive):

- `rg "list_build_runs_result|domain/build-runs" src tests changelogs docs`
  — the only caller is `src/api/routers/internal/domain.py:594`
  (`GET /domain/build-runs`); the only frontend fetcher of that path is
  `src/front/static/domain/js/domain-runs.js:175` (`_loadBuildRuns`, out of
  scope per the task's constraints — not modified, only read).
- Read `_loadBuildRuns` in full: it destructures only `data.success`,
  `data.message`, and `data.runs` from the response. No reference to
  `data.versions` or `data.current_version` anywhere in the file.
- `rg "runsCache\.versions|data\.versions|data\.current_version" src/front/static/domain/js/domain-runs.js`
  — no matches.
- `rg "list_build_runs_result|domain/build-runs" tests` — only
  `tests/units/front/test_runs_page.py:149` asserts the fetch URL string;
  no test reads `versions` / `current_version` off this response.
- Verified `/settings/build-runs/{domain_name}` (`build-analytics.js`,
  `settings.py`) is a wholly separate endpoint backed by
  `config_service.get_build_runs_result`, not `Domain.list_build_runs_result`
  — irrelevant to this removal.
- Verified `list_versions_sorted` (`RegistryService.py:733`) is a pure
  read-only registry query with no side effects, so dropping the call has
  no consequence beyond the removed round-trip.

Conclusion: both fields were genuinely unconsumed by any caller. Removed.

